# Reference reconstruction v35

Unapproved built-in image generation concept. User permits inference of obscured reference clouds. Generated reconstruction is not an exact trace or deterministic coordinate conversion. First output had inverted crowns and was rejected; final output corrects crowns inward, bases outward. Long tangential tails remain more pronounced than reference. Not packed opacity/material data. No level replacement.

## Initial prompt

Create a faithful cloud reconstruction and polar projection from this screenshot. The user explicitly wants each reference cloud's actual shape, not new clouds merely in its style. Ignore and remove all lettering, house, people, terrain, QR and logos; infer only cloud portions occluded by them.
COPY visible silhouette details and large shadow shapes as closely as possible: a low horizontal distant cloud strip on left with several small unequal humps and long thin flat trailing layers; center-right a tall broad irregular cloud tower with flatter crown, a deep saddle separating it from huge far-right sloping shoulder; huge lower right shadow mass has extremely sparse irregular pale accents. Copy these individual shapes and proportions. No replacement with a row of equal round cumulus balls. NO cauliflower noise, repetitive scalloped marks, decorative brushwork. Broad nearly solid grayscale planes, reference's quiet painted animation look.
Now geometrically wrap that reconstructed planar horizontal cloud strip around a polar annulus. This must resemble a Cartesian-to-polar image transformation of the original traced clouds, NOT manual placement of cloud modules. The original horizontal axis is ANGLE around center. The original vertical axis is RADIUS, original tops at smaller radius and original bottoms at larger radius. A flat horizontal base or long horizontal tail in reference becomes a CONCENTRIC ARC around center at constant radius. ALL TAILS bend tangentially around the circumference, NEVER point into center or remain horizontal across the square. On right a tail runs vertically along arc; at upper-right it runs diagonally along arc; bottom tails run horizontally along arc. The shadow boundaries undergo the same exact bend. Original tallest right reference cloud occupies right side of polar atlas, smaller low reference clouds occupy left-to-bottom sectors; maintain unequal original scale. Keep broad black central sky opening and black outside margin.
Reconstruct the few original large cloud shapes and their long bases, not an ornamental evenly scalloped wreath. Inward-facing crowns have reference's irregular broad plateaus and small low relief bumps; outward bases shaded and slightly softened. Large quiet gray shadow planes; pale gray/off-white filled light planes. No white outline, detached strokes, doubled borders, checkerboard, sun, stars, text or labels. Output only one square grayscale polar cloud artwork on opaque pure black.

## Correction

The last image is radially INSIDE-OUT. Fix only this radial inversion, preserving its broad reference-like flat gray shadow shapes and sparse long tangential tails. On the RIGHT the pale tall rounded cloud CROWNS must protrude LEFT into the central black opening, while the broad dark flat cloud BASE must lie along OUTER RIGHT circumference. At TOP the crowns must protrude DOWN into center and dark base UP toward perimeter. At BOTTOM crowns must point UP and dark base DOWN. At LEFT crowns point RIGHT and base LEFT. Radially reflect the cloud band's cross-section: move the irregular pale crown boundary currently facing exterior to the INNER side, and move smooth dark base boundary currently facing central hole to the OUTER side. Not a whole-image rotation or horizontal mirror. Keep all tails tangential, curving along circumference, not radial. Preserve asymmetric giant right mass and small low left clouds. Clean black central opening with irregular inward cloud crown silhouette, no circular dark wall bordering center. Gray shadow plains stay near outside. No new cauliflower details, no repeated balls, no outlines, no text. Square grayscale artwork on black.
